= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <compactness theorem> for <first-order logic> says that a set $\Sigma$ of <first-order sentences> has a model if and only if every finite subset of $\Sigma$ has a model. It applies to arbitrary languages, including expansions by arbitrarily many new constants. Equivalently, if $\Sigma$ entails a sentence, some finite subset already entails it. We will use both formulations with the <diagram of a structure>.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Name every element $a\in A$ by a new constant $c_a$, distinct from the symbols already in $L'$. Let $\operatorname{Diag}(A)$ be its full atomic and negated atomic <diagram of a structure>. A model of this diagram contains an isomorphic copy of $A$ as an $L$-<substructure>. We claim
$$
T'\cup\operatorname{Diag}(A)
$$
is satisfiable.

Otherwise <compactness theorem> gives a finite diagram conjunction $\delta(c_{a_1},\ldots,c_{a_m})$ inconsistent with $T'$. Replace these new constants by variables. Since $T'$ says nothing about the new names, it then entails the <universal sentence>
$$
\forall x_1\cdots\forall x_m\,\neg\delta(x_1,\ldots,x_m).
$$
The conjunction $\delta$ uses only symbols of $L$, so this is one of the universal $L$-consequences assumed true in $A$. But $A$ satisfies $\delta(a_1,\ldots,a_m)$, a contradiction. A model of the consistent enlarged theory gives **an embedding of $A$ into the $L$-reduct of a model of $T'$**. Negated atomic facts and disequalities in the diagram ensure that this is a <structure embedding>, not merely a homomorphism.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $U$ be all <universal sentences> true in $C$. The theory $T\cup U$ is consistent. Indeed, if a finite conjunction $u$ of sentences from $U$ were inconsistent with $T$, then $T\models\neg u$. A finite conjunction of <universal sentences> is universal, so its negation is equivalent to an <existential sentence>. By the hypothesis $C$ would satisfy $\neg u$, whereas it satisfies $u$.

Choose $A\models T\cup U$. Now expand the language by names for every element of $C$ and consider its <elementary diagram> $\operatorname{ElDiag}(C)$. Its universal consequences in the original language are exactly $U$: any such consequence is true in the named copy of $C$, while any original-language sentence true in $C$ already belongs to its elementary diagram. Part (b), applied to $A$ and this expanded theory, gives a <structure embedding> of $A$ into a model $D$ of $\operatorname{ElDiag}(C)$. The named copy of $C$ is an <elementary substructure> of $D$. Identifying that copy with $C$, we obtain
$$
\boxed{A\models T,\qquad A\hookrightarrow D,\qquad C\preccurlyeq D.}
$$
Thus the required target is an <elementary extension> of $C$, although $C$ itself need not be a model of $T$.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Let $S$ be the set of all <existential sentences> entailed by $T$. Certainly every model of $T$ satisfies $S$. Conversely, take $C\models S$. Part (c) gives $A\models T$ embedded in an <elementary extension> $D$ of $C$. After identifying $A$ with its image, $D$ is a superstructure of a model of $T$. The assumed closure under superstructures implies $D\models T$. Since $C\preccurlyeq D$, it follows that $C\models T$ as well. Hence
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Mod}(T)=\operatorname{Mod}(S),\qquad S\subseteq\exists_1.}
$$
This supplies an existential axiomatization. Conversely, an <existential sentence> is preserved under embeddings because its finite tuple of witnesses and the quantifier-free facts about them persist, explaining why this is the appropriate syntactic class.

