For a relational <first-order structure> $A$, form its <Gaifman graph>: distinct elements are adjacent when they occur together in a tuple of some interpreted relation. Let $d_A$ be graph distance, with distance infinite between different components. For a tuple $\mathbf a=(a_1,\ldots,a_m)$, its radius-$r$ neighbourhood has universe
$$
N_r^A(\mathbf a)=\{b\in A:\min_i d_A(b,a_i)\le r\}.
$$
The neighbourhood structure is the induced relational structure on this set, expanded by constants marking the entries of $\mathbf a$ in their original order. Its isomorphism type thus records the centres, not just the unmarked ball. For one binary graph relation, this is the usual union of the radius-$r$ graph balls. This formulation assumes the finite relational signatures used in the finite-model setting.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

An exact-count form of the <Hanf locality lemma> is as follows. For every <quantifier rank> $q$, put $r=3^q$. If finite relational structures $A,B$ admit a bijection $f:A\to B$ such that
$$
(N_r^A(a),a)\cong(N_r^B(f(a)),f(a))\qquad\text{for every }a\in A,
$$
then $A$ and $B$ agree on every <first-order sentence> of <quantifier rank> at most $q$. Equivalently, they have the same number of occurrences of every marked radius-$r$ one-point neighbourhood type. The individual local isomorphisms need not be restrictions of $f$.

A bounded-degree threshold version permits the type counts to be different when both exceed a sufficiently large bound depending on the degree bound and $q$. We need only the exact-count form. Its locality content is that sufficiently large matching neighbourhoods and their multiplicities determine all sentences up to the prescribed <quantifier rank>.

= c
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Suppose a <first-order sentence> $\theta$ defined the <bipartite graphs>, equivalently the 2-colourable graphs. Let $q$ be its <quantifier rank> and $r=3^q$. Choose an odd integer $N>2r+1$, and compare
$$
A=C_{2N},\qquad B=C_N\sqcup C_N.
$$
Both have $2N$ vertices. Every marked radius-$r$ neighbourhood in either graph is a path with $2r+1$ vertices and its middle vertex marked: the choice of $N$ prevents the ball from wrapping around a cycle. Thus every vertex has exactly the same neighbourhood type in both graphs, and that type occurs $2N$ times in each. Any bijection of their universes meets the <Hanf locality lemma> hypothesis.

Therefore $A$ and $B$ agree on $\theta$. But $A$ is an even cycle and is bipartite, whereas $B$ contains odd cycles and is not bipartite. This contradiction proves
$$
\boxed{\text{No first-order sentence defines 2-colourability, even on finite graphs.}}
$$
The equal total size is useful here: no large-count threshold or comparison of unequal multiplicities is needed.

= 7
{parent=Paper 23}
{scope}

= a
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

<Fagin's theorem> states that, for a finite relational signature, an isomorphism-invariant class of finite structures has its standard encoding language in <NP> if and only if it is definable by an <existential second-order logic> sentence
$$
\exists R_1\cdots\exists R_m\,\psi,
$$
where $\psi$ is first-order and the relation variables have fixed finite arities. The forward implication includes the substantive encoding of a polynomially bounded accepting computation by relations. In the reverse implication the relations are polynomial-length certificates and the fixed <first-order sentence> can be checked in <polynomial time>. This is a finite-structure characterization; no order need be named in the input signature.

= b
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Here equivalence means equivalence on all finite structures of the chosen signature, and an <existential sentence> means an existential second-order sentence. Suppose, towards a contradiction, that <P> equals <NP>. We use that P is closed under complementation, that fixed first-order model checking is polynomial-time, and that guessing a polynomial-length string followed by a polynomial-time test is in NP.

Under this equality, evaluating any fixed <second-order logic> sentence is in P. Prove this by induction through its quantifiers and Boolean operations. The first-order part is already polynomial-time. If an inner formula can be evaluated in P, existentially quantifying a relation of fixed arity guesses at most $n^k$ bits and then performs the inner polynomial-time test. This gives an NP procedure and hence, under the assumption, a P procedure. A universal relation quantifier is handled by complementing the corresponding existential test. Boolean combinations remain polynomial-time as well. A fixed formula has only finitely many such steps and fixed arities, so the resulting time bound is polynomial.

Consequently every second-order definable class lies in P, hence in NP. By <Fagin's theorem> it has an equivalent existential second-order sentence. This contradicts the posited second-order sentence with no such equivalent. Therefore
$$
\boxed{P\ne NP.}
$$
The proof uses the equality assumption to collapse quantifier alternation; it does not assume an unproved strictness statement about the polynomial hierarchy.

= c
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Every fixed <second-order logic> sentence is evaluable in <PSPACE>. On a universe of size $n$, a relation of arity $k$ is stored using $n^k$ bits. Enumerate its possible interpretations with a counter of the same polynomial length, recursively evaluate the inner formula and reuse the storage. Universal quantifiers require checking all interpretations; existential ones stop at the first success. The fixed quantifier depth and fixed arities bound total space by a polynomial, even though time can be exponential. First-order quantifiers and Boolean operations require no larger bound.

If $NP=PSPACE$, this places the class defined by the assumed second-order sentence in NP. <Fagin's theorem> would give an equivalent existential second-order sentence, again contradicting the hypothesis. Hence
$$
\boxed{NP\ne PSPACE.}
$$
For completeness, $NP\subseteq PSPACE$: a polynomial-time nondeterministic computation can be searched deterministically depth first, retaining a polynomial-length computation branch and reusing space. Thus the inequality is a strict containment under the stated hypothesis.

= 8
{parent=Paper 23}
{scope}

= a
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Fix a <partial fixed-point logic> formula $\phi(R,\mathbf x)$, with $R$ of arity $k$. On a finite ordered structure, an interpretation of $R$ is a bit string of length $n^k$. To decide whether some interpretation makes $\phi$ true at a given tuple, enumerate all these strings and evaluate $\phi$ for each. Evaluation uses <polynomial space> by the permitted characterization of PFP, and the enumerating counter also has polynomial length. Storage is reused between interpretations. Thus the resulting pointed-structure property is decidable in <PSPACE>.

Apply the assumed PFP characterization of PSPACE on ordered structures to this property. It gives a PFP formula $\psi(\mathbf x)$ such that
$$
\boxed{\psi(\mathbf x)\equiv\exists R\,\phi(R,\mathbf x)\quad\text{on finite ordered structures}.}
$$
To justify the formula version from a sentence characterization, temporarily name the tuple by finitely many new constants, apply the characterization in that expanded finite signature, and replace those constants by the free variables, renaming bound variables to avoid capture. This does not introduce a new order assumption: the input already interprets $<$ as a <linear order>. Multiple quantified relations are handled by repeated application.

= b
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

As usual, a class of structures here is closed under isomorphism and uses standard finite-structure encodings. First suppose $K$ is defined by $\exists R\,\phi$, with $\phi$ in <partial fixed-point logic>. Deterministically enumerate the polynomial-length encodings of $R$ and evaluate $\phi$ on each expansion. PFP evaluation is in <PSPACE> even without a supplied order: a relation of fixed arity requires polynomially many bits, iteration has at most exponentially many states, and a polynomial-length counter suffices to stop after that many stages. If no consecutive stages agree before a repeat must have occurred, the partial fixed point is empty. Nested fixed points have fixed syntactic depth and can be evaluated with reused polynomial storage. Thus the encoding language of $K$ is in PSPACE.

Conversely, suppose that encoding language is decidable in PSPACE. Expand each structure by a freely chosen <linear order> $R$. On such ordered expansions, the assumed characterization gives a PFP sentence $\psi(R)$ deciding whether the reduct belongs to $K$. Since $K$ is isomorphism-invariant, the answer is independent of the particular chosen order. Hence
$$
\boxed{A\in K\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
A\models\exists R\,[\operatorname{Lin}(R)\land\psi(R)].}
$$
The expression inside the second-order quantifier is PFP, since the assertion that $R$ is a <linear order> is first-order. Every finite universe has a <linear order>. This proves both directions, using an existentially supplied order when the original signature has none. The construction is also the order-independent consequence of part (a), which shows that existential relation quantification does not increase PFP's power on an already ordered structure.

= 9
{parent=Paper 23}
{scope}

= a
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For an unrestricted binary relation $R$, the extension axioms permit arbitrary directed edges and loops. For each $m\ge0$ and each choice of bits $\epsilon_i,\delta_i,\eta\in\{0,1\}$, require that every tuple of distinct $x_1,\ldots,x_m$ has a fresh element $y$ with
$$
y\ne x_i\quad\text{for all }i,\qquad
R(y,y)^\eta\land\bigwedge_{i=1}^m
\bigl(R(y,x_i)^{\epsilon_i}\land R(x_i,y)^{\delta_i}\bigr).
$$
Here $P^1$ denotes $P$ and $P^0$ denotes $\neg P$; the notation is a literal abbreviation, not exponentiation of a relation. Incoming and outgoing choices are independent, and the loop bit is also specified. These are the <extension axioms for an unrestricted binary relation>. If one instead restricts to undirected loop-free graphs, the allowable patterns reduce to the random-graph extension axioms of Question 2; no such restriction is imposed here.

= b
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $T_e$ be the theory of the <extension axioms for an unrestricted binary relation>. It has <quantifier elimination>. To prove this, consider a conjunction of literals $\theta(y,\mathbf x)$. A witness $y$ equal to one of the parameters is handled by substitution. A witness distinct from them is governed entirely by a finite pattern of incoming edges, outgoing edges and its loop. For each equality pattern of the parameter tuple, incompatible literals make the pattern impossible, and every compatible pattern is supplied by the appropriate extension axiom. Thus existence of a witness is a finite disjunction of quantifier-free conditions on the parameters. Disjunctive normal form and induction on formulas eliminate all remaining quantifiers.

Given any formula $\phi(\mathbf x)$, choose a quantifier-free $\psi(\mathbf x)$ with
$$
T_e\models\forall\mathbf x\,[\phi(\mathbf x)\leftrightarrow\psi(\mathbf x)].
$$
By <compactness theorem>, finitely many extension axioms $\sigma_1,\ldots,\sigma_s$ already entail this particular universally quantified equivalence. Each has asymptotic probability one by the assumption. The union bound gives
$$
\Pr\!\left(\bigwedge_{j=1}^s\sigma_j\right)
\ge1-\sum_{j=1}^s\Pr(\neg\sigma_j)\longrightarrow1.
$$
Therefore the universal equivalence also has asymptotic probability one. We obtain **asymptotic equivalence to a <quantifier-free formula>**, uniformly over every tuple in each structure satisfying those finitely many axioms. An infinite conjunction of probability-one events is not used; the finite consequence supplied by compactness is essential.

= 10
{parent=Paper 23}
{scope}

= a
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use only the variable names $x,y$. Define mutually renamed formulas by
$$
D_1^x(x)=(x=x),\qquad
D_{m+1}^x(x)=\exists y\,[x<y\land D_m^y(y)],
$$
where $D_m^y$ is obtained from $D_m^x$ by globally interchanging $x$ and $y$, including their bound occurrences. By induction, $D_m^x(a)$ means that an increasing chain of $m$ elements starts at $a$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\lambda_n=\exists x\,D_n^x(x)\in L^2}
$$
holds exactly when the <linear order> has at least $n$ elements. Inner quantifiers may bind the name $x$ again, but their scope is only the nested subformula; the comparison $x<y$ still refers to the preceding point. This <finite-variable logic> construction works on finite and infinite <linear orders>.

= b
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Choose a variable name $y\ne x$ among the $k$ available names and define
$$
C_1^x(x)=\phi(x),\qquad
C_{m+1}^x(x)=\phi(x)\land\exists y\,[x<y\land C_m^y(y)].
$$
The formula $C_m^y$ is obtained by interchanging the names $x,y$ throughout $C_m^x$, including bound occurrences. Its only free variable is $y$. A permutation of variable names preserves membership in $L^k$, so every formula in this recursion still uses at most the original $k$ names. In particular, bound variables inside a copy of $\phi$ do not accidentally capture the previous comparison: their scope stays inside that copy.

Induction proves that $C_m^x(a)$ holds exactly when there is an increasing $m$-tuple of satisfying elements whose first entry is $a$. Any set of $m$ distinct points in a <linear order> can be put in increasing order. Therefore the <variable-recycling count on a linear order>
$$
\boxed{\phi_n=\exists x\,C_n^x(x)\in L^k}
$$
is the required sentence. This does not use counting quantifiers or additional variable names, and it applies equally to infinite <linear orders>.

= c
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

We use the finite-structure convention of Section B. Express that $<$ is a strict <linear order> by irreflexivity, transitivity and comparability of distinct points. Express that $E$ is an <equivalence relation> by reflexivity, symmetry and transitivity. All these assertions use at most the three variable names $x,y,z$.

In a finite linearly ordered structure, every <equivalence class> has a unique least element. The two-variable formula
$$
\operatorname{Rep}(x)=\forall y\,[y<x\longrightarrow\neg E(y,x)]
$$
selects exactly those least elements. Apply part (b) to this formula, using $k=3$, to obtain $\operatorname{Rep}_n\in L^3$ saying that at least $n$ representatives exist. The required sentence is
$$
\boxed{\eta_n=\operatorname{Lin}(<)\land\operatorname{Eq}(E)\land\operatorname{Rep}_n.}
$$
This gives the <least representatives of finite equivalence classes> construction. Under the two structural axioms, counting representatives is exactly counting <equivalence classes>.

Finiteness is essential to the assertion as written in this section. If it were interpreted as applying to all infinite structures, the general claim would fail, not merely this choice of representatives. Partition the rational order into three dense classes, or into four dense classes, and let $E$ mean belonging to the same class. In the three-pebble version of the <Ehrenfeucht-Fraïssé game>, the duplicator can play indefinitely between these structures: after one pebble is moved, at most two old classes remain represented; a new point can be placed in the required interval in its corresponding old class, or in a class different from both. Density makes every required placement possible in either structure. Thus the structures agree on all $L^3$ sentences although one has three classes and the other four. The finite reading of the printed request is therefore necessary for $n=4$.
